---
layout: base.njk
wide: true
pagination:
  data: collections.shelves
  size: 1
  alias: shelf
permalink: "/shelf/{{ shelf.category }}/"
eleventyComputed:
  title: "{{ shelf.label }}"
---
{% set items = collections.subjectPages | inCategory(shelf.category) | shelveSort | shelfRanks %}

<h1>{{ shelf.label }}</h1>

{#- Sort, in CSS. The radios carry no script: each tile ships a precomputed rank
    per axis (see shelfRanks) and the checked radio decides which one `order`
    reads. DOM order is status order, so the default view needs no rule at all
    and a reader with no CSS still gets the shelf in its intended sequence.

    The caveat worth knowing: `order` moves tiles visually but not in the DOM, so
    tab and screen-reader order stay as authored. That is why the default is the
    authored one — only the three opt-in axes diverge, and a reader who never
    touches the control is never out of sync.

    Hidden below six items: a shelf that fits on one screen is not one you sort,
    and the boardgames shelf currently holds exactly one game. -#}
{#- The wrapper is what :has() hangs off — the radios and the grid have to sit
    inside one element for the checked state to reach the tiles. -#}
<div class="shelf-sorter">
{% if items | length > 5 %}
<fieldset class="shelf-sort">
  <legend>sort</legend>
  <input type="radio" name="shelf-sort" id="sort-status" checked>
  <label for="sort-status">status</label>
  <input type="radio" name="shelf-sort" id="sort-az">
  <label for="sort-az">a–z</label>
  <input type="radio" name="shelf-sort" id="sort-recent">
  <label for="sort-recent">recent</label>
  <input type="radio" name="shelf-sort" id="sort-fav">
  <label for="sort-fav">favorites</label>
</fieldset>
{% endif %}

{#- Two jobs, split across two pieces of art. The COVER identifies: a reader
    hunting for one game scans box art far faster than titles, and faster still
    than a dozen abstract night scenes that deliberately encode no identity.
    The BANNER above it is that subject's own sky cropped to a strip, and it
    keeps both axes the tile always carried — how much aurora is left says
    status, how brightly it burns says how recently it was written about. The
    shelf still reads as a state-of-the-category at a glance; you just no longer
    have to decode which scene is which game to use it.

    Covers are SOURCED, and sourced means sometimes absent. A subject without
    one falls back to the whole generated tile, stretched by CSS to the exact
    height the banner-and-cover stack comes to — so the original guarantee holds
    (no shelf goes blank waiting on an image) and a half-covered shelf still
    lays out as one even grid rather than a ragged one. -#}
<ul class="shelf-grid">
  {% for item in items %}
  <li class="shelf-item{% if item.favorite %} is-favorite{% endif %}" style="--o-az:{{ item.oAz }};--o-recent:{{ item.oRecent }};--o-fav:{{ item.oFav }}">
    <a href="/s/{{ item.slug }}/">
      <span class="shelf-art">
        {% if item.coverUrl %}
        <span class="sky sky-banner" style="background-image:url({{ item.bannerUrl }})" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        {#- A real <img>, unlike every sky on the site: the generated art is
            decoration and belongs in CSS, but a cover is content, it wants an
            alt, and a shelf that keeps growing wants lazy loading. -#}
        <img class="shelf-cover" src="{{ item.coverUrl }}" alt="{{ item.title }} cover art" loading="lazy" decoding="async">
        {% else %}
        <span class="sky" style="background-image:url({{ item.skyUrl }})" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        {% endif %}
      </span>
      <span class="shelf-caption">{{ item.title }}</span>
      {#- Gold is the fast channel, but it is only a channel — this badge is what
          carries the same fact for a reader who cannot see the colour, and it is
          why the frame does not have to work alone. -#}
      {% if item.favorite %}<span class="badge b-favorite">favorite</span>{% endif %}
      <span class="badge b-{{ item.status }}">{{ item.status }}</span>
      {% if item.rating %}<span class="rating">{{ item.rating }}/5</span>{% endif %}
    </a>
  </li>
  {% endfor %}
</ul>
</div>
